{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790459024,"nonce":"6SBVxZjYe2wAJdLWJrxLxX94k3k=","title":"GPU plugin on wasm: WebGPU on a GPU agent, driven through shared memory by a binding generated from WebIDL","message":"On wasm32-wasip1-threads a caribou program runs on Web Workers over one\nshared memory (krio; ash docs/internals/wasm-threads.md). The agent\nrunning the program never returns to its event loop (ash\nbrowser/canvas.rs), so it can neither see a WebGPU Promise resolve nor\npresent a frame. wgpu's webgpu backend is no route here either: it rides\nwasm-bindgen and web-sys, for wasm32-unknown-unknown.\n\nSo WebGPU lives on a GPU agent (a worker holding the GPUDevice and an\nOffscreenCanvas, or the page), and the plugin drives it remotely:\n- caribou-bindgen generates, from the WebIDL of the operations gpu.api.rs\n  uses, an encoder for the plugin side (each call as interface,\n  operation, handle and marshalled arguments into a queue in shared\n  memory) and a decoder in JS for the GPU agent (runs it against its own\n  objects in a handle table). This extends ead67cc.\n- A result, and a Promise's settlement, come back through a shared slot:\n  the calling fiber parks on it (atomic wait), and the agent notifies.\n  requestAdapter, requestDevice and mapAsync block only their fiber.\n- Uploads (writeBuffer, writeTexture, mapped ranges) are addresses in\n  shared memory, read by the agent without copying out.\n- The agent presents on its own clock.\nParent: 7885628.","files":null}]}